# Build-lane OWNERSHIP LEASE (scripts/lane-lease.sh, read by scripts/sweep-build-lanes.sh).
# Remote per-lane runtime state, never source — but this line is also LOAD-BEARING, not
# hygiene. `scripts/pbuild` syncs with `rsync -az --delete --filter=':- .gitignore'`, and
# the lease lives at the ROOT of that destination (`<lane>/.pbuild-lease`). An rsync
# exclude is a receiver-side PROTECT rule as well as a sender-side hide, so this entry is
# the ONLY thing standing between a live lease and the next pbuild call. MEASURED
# 2026-07-26 with this line absent, on the real tree and the real command:
#   rsync -az --delete --dry-run -i --exclude='.git/' --filter=':- .gitignore' ./ persvati:dregg-build/crewbraid/
#   → *deleting   .pbuild-lease
# i.e. agent B's build would silently strip agent A's claim and the next sweep would then
# see an unleased lane and delete its artifacts. Do not remove this line.
.pbuild-lease

# scripts/feature-t3-sweep.sh's per-pair record. A measurement artifact, never source: the
# COMMITTED half of that sweep is scripts/feature-t3-known-red.tsv (the ratchet).
/feature-t3-sweep.tsv
/t3-shard*.tsv

# Atlas screenshots are large, regenerable-by-hand UI captures. They were 40 MB of
# LFS-tracked binary that every checkout re-pulled — which is what exhausted the LFS
# BANDWIDTH budget and made the entire site undeployable. They are not shipped; the
# site build generates a labeled placeholder wherever one is referenced.
dregg-atlas/site/screenshots/

